<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · other</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yanagawa affair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1635</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Yanagawa ikken</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yanagawa affair was a dispute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the 1630s. Yanagawa Shigeoki, a senior retainer of the Sō lords of Tsushima, revealed that the Sō had forged and altered diplomatic letters exchanged between the Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> and the kings of Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in order to restore relations after Hideyoshi's invasions. In 1635 the shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemitsu">Tokugawa Iemitsu</a> ruled in favour of the Sō, exiled Yanagawa and left Korean relations in their hands.</p></div>
